s aimed to do?", "a": "To prepare students for their intended careers, although its structure has changed to meet the demands of a dynamic job market."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.2 · What must students do to earn a degree?", "a": "Fulfil the requirements of their chosen programme at an institution (university, community college or specialised school), including passing exams."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.2 · Compulsory and elective courses?", "a": "Some courses are compulsory and others are elective, so students can tailor their education to their interests."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.2 · Why are community colleges popular? Which degrees do they offer?", "a": "They cost less and suit students who are unsure of their major. Their two-year programmes lead to a certificate, an associate degree, or transfer to a four-year university."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.2 · Most common institution for a bachelor's? Duration?", "a": "Universities. There are over 2,300 four-year institutions, and students typically spend four years there."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.2 · What does an advanced degree allow? What does it require?", "a": "It opens doors to more specialised careers. It requires significant dedication, critical thinking and extensive research."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.2 · Names of undergraduate years.", "a": "Freshman (1st year), sophomore (2nd), junior (3rd), senior (4th)."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.2 · Length of the academic year and number of semesters.", "a": "About nine months, with two semesters per year. Students take four or five courses per semester, and 120–130 credit hours are needed for a bachelor's degree."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.2 · What is MIT? Reputation? Pace of learning?", "a": "The Massachusetts Institute of Technology, named the number one university in the world in the QS ranking. It has a stellar reputation for academic excellence and rigorous demands; the pace is likened to \"drinking from a firehose\"."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.2 · Admission to MIT? Which students?", "a": "Admission is highly competitive. MIT seeks exceptional students who are academically proficient and show initiative and intellectual curiosity."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.2 · What does MIT focus on?", "a": "On personal growth and individual learning styles. Information is not \"spoon-fed\", so students must find their own way of learning, which prepares them for real-world problems that need unconventional approaches."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.3 · Types of institutions in Russia?", "a": "State and non-state accredited institutions: national universities (MSU, SPbSU), federal universities, national research universities, universities, academies and institutes."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.3 · Admission criteria?", "a": "Admission is competitive and based on Unified State Exam (USE) results. Applicants must show high performance in subjects related to the speciality, such as maths, physics and computer science."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.3 · Qualifications under the 2012 Law on Education?", "a": "Bachelor's, Master's and Specialist's diplomas."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.3 · Duration of Bachelor's and Master's?", "a": "Bachelor's: four years full-time. Master's: usually two years."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.3 · Options for working students?", "a": "Part-time and e-learning (correspondence) study. They take more time to complete."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.3 · Subjects in the curriculum?", "a": "Core subjects such as maths and physics, plus humanities: history, philosophy and foreign languages. The programmes are practice-oriented, with an emphasis on fundamental engineering disciplines."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.3 · Careers for Bachelor's graduates?", "a": "They can work as engineers, project managers or technical consultants in various industries, or continue to a master's."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.3 · What is a specialist degree?", "a": "A traditional Russian form of higher education lasting five years. It gives comprehensive, specialised training in engineering disciplines."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.3 · Final requirements?", "a": "Each stage ends with a degree paper (graduation thesis) and a final state examination."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.3 · Postgraduate programmes?", "a": "Usually 3–4 years, leading to Candidate of Science and Doctor of Science degrees, awarded after a public defence of a thesis showing original research."}, {"u": "2", "q": "2.3 · Talk about your studies. (Sample answer, adapt to your case)", "a": "I am a first-year student at [university], studying [speciality]. I am pursuing a Bachelor's degree, which takes four years. The programme includes maths, physics and engineering disciplines, plus foreign languages. After graduating I can work as an engineer, project manager or technical consultant, or continue to a master's degree."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.1 · Gutenberg's revolutionary concept? How long did the press stay unchanged?", "a": "Movable type: individual metal characters that could be arranged and rearranged to form words, sentences and whole blocks of text. The mechanics of the press stayed almost unchanged for 300 years."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.1 · Why did early presses wear down?", "a": "They required great force to operate, so the wood cracked and the metal type wore down."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.1 · Inventor of the light bulb? Key elements of his success?", "a": "Thomas Edison (1879), although many inventors tried before him. His version won thanks to effective materials, a higher vacuum and high resistance, which made centralised power distribution economically viable. He also developed inventions that made bulbs practical."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.1 · The Wright brothers' breakthrough?", "a": "On 17 December 1903 they made the first successful flight of a powered airplane, the Flyer. It took off, flew at even speed and landed without damage. Orville's flight lasted 12 seconds."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.1 · What preceded the first airplane?", "a": "Kites, balloons and gliders."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.1 · Niépce's invention?", "a": "A French inventor who created a prototype photographic camera in 1816. In 1826 he took the oldest surviving photograph, showing the view from a window of his house."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.1 · Carl Benz's breakthrough?", "a": "The German engineer patented a gas-powered car engine (1885), which began the era of the automobile and led to the Mercedes-Benz company."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.1 · Henry Ford's contribution?", "a": "He used mass-production techniques, including the assembly line, to make cars affordable, paving the way for the global automotive industry."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.1 · Who was Alexander Graham Bell?", "a": "A Scottish-born inventor, fascinated by sound from childhood. His father and grandfather worked with speech and elocution, and his mother lost her hearing. He moved to Boston and became a teacher of the deaf. He invented the telephone, later experimented with other machines, and helped found the National Geographic Society."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.1 · Why is 10 March 1876 important?", "a": "On that day Bell made the first successful telephone call to his assistant Watson (\"Mr. Watson, come here\"). He had patented the invention days earlier because other inventors were racing to build the same thing."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.2 · What is Alfred Nobel famous for?", "a": "He invented dynamite, an important explosive and a breakthrough in chemistry. He made his fortune from explosives."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.2 · How was the Nobel Prize established?", "a": "When Nobel died, he left most of his wealth in a trust to fund what became the Nobel Prizes."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.2 · Selection process?", "a": "Every year thousands of scientists and professors are invited to nominate candidates. Committees choose the laureates. Each winner is invited to a ceremony in Stockholm to receive a medal, a diploma and a sum of money."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.2 · Fields?", "a": "Physics, Chemistry, Physiology or Medicine, Literature, Peace and Economics. Mathematics and Law are not Nobel fields."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.2 · Maximum number of laureates? Exception?", "a": "Up to three people can share one award. The exception is organisations: the Red Cross has won the Peace Prize three times."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.2 · Famous laureates?", "a": "Ernest Hemingway (The Old Man and the Sea), Alexander Fleming (penicillin), Albert Einstein (photoelectric effect), Martin Luther King Jr. (peaceful struggle), Marie Curie (two Nobel Prizes: physics and chemistry), and the Red Cross (help for prisoners of war)."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.2 · What is Alan Turing famous for?", "a": "Breaking the Nazi Enigma code in WWII, which helped end the war sooner and advanced cryptography. He also introduced the Turing Machine, helped design early computers (ACE) and explored machine learning."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.2 · The Turing Machine?", "a": "Introduced in 1936, it is a theoretical model of how computers process information. It is still essential for understanding algorithms and programming."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.2 · The Turing Test?", "a": "A test of a machine's ability to exhibit intelligent behaviour. It remains a cornerstone of discussions about artificial intelligence."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.2 · The Turing Award?", "a": "Called the \"Nobel Prize of Computing\", it was created in 1966 by the ACM (founded 1947). It honours groundbreaking contributions in AI, programming languages, databases, operating systems and cryptography. Since 2014 it carries a $1 million prize sponsored by Google. Winners include John McCarthy (AI, LISP) and Donald Knuth (algorithms)."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.3 · Original Coca-Cola recipe and the shroud of mystery?", "a": "John Pemberton created the drink in 1886 and shared the recipe with only a few people. Asa Candler bought the rights in 1891. The recipe was passed on by word of mouth and first written down in 1919, when investors needed collateral for a loan."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.3 · Where is the formula kept? Security?", "a": "In a vault in the World of Coca-Cola museum in Atlanta (earlier in an Atlanta bank). Access needs a palm scanner, a numerical code pad and a huge steel door. Inside is a safe box with a metal case holding the recipe. Only two senior executives know the formula, and they never travel together."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.3 · Purpose of the mystery?", "a": "It is a marketing strategy meant to attract attention to the product."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.3 · What was the US Industrial Revolution based on?", "a": "On intellectual property smuggled out of England."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.3 · What did the water-powered spinning frame change?", "a": "Arkwright's water frame (1771) increased production of cotton goods tenfold and let English factories keep up with demand for textiles."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.3 · British laws?", "a": "In 1774 Parliament passed laws to protect industrial secrets, forbidding engineers and mechanics to travel abroad."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.3 · How did the water frame reach the USA?", "a": "Samuel Slater, who had worked in an English factory, memorised the plans and sailed to America in 1789. He built a factory in Rhode Island."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.3 · The Rhode Island System?", "a": "The model of factory life Slater created in 1803: a village (Slaterville) built around the mill, with tenements for workers and a company store."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.3 · Slater and Lowell in common?", "a": "Both visited English factories, memorised the plans of a machine (the water frame and the power loom) and brought them to America, where they used them in their own mills."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.3 · What does know-how refer to?", "a": "Practical knowledge or technical secrets of how to do something, such as a recipe, process or method, that gives a competitive advantage."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.3 · Why take advertising with a grain of salt?", "a": "Because the information often comes from the company's own claims and is part of a marketing campaign. Coke's story about the formula may be exaggerated."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.3 · Purpose of smuggling? Why does it still exist?", "a": "To get secrets, technologies or goods illegally and gain an economic or strategic advantage. It still exists because information is valuable and competition between companies and countries remains. Today it appears as industrial and cyber espionage."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.3 · What is the brain drain?", "a": "The emigration of highly skilled people (scientists, engineers) to other countries, because of better pay, working conditions, research funding and career prospects abroad."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.3 · Trade secrets.", "a": "A trade secret protects intellectual property by keeping it secret, without registration. Categories: recipes, processes, designs, methods of making products, and compilations of information. Examples: the Coca-Cola formula, KFC's blend of eleven herbs and spices, Google's search algorithm. Stealing or publishing them is illegal; the government equivalent is \"classified\" information."}, {"u": "4", "q": "4.3 · Top Russian inventions.", "a": "Caterpillar tracks: Zagryazhsky (1837), then Blinov (1877), leading to tractors and tanks. Electric railway wagons: Pirotsky (1870s); the first electric tram line opened in Berlin in 1881 based on his designs. Videotape recorder: Alexander Poniatoff (Ampex). Radio: Alexander Popov (1895), with Marconi's competing claim. Helicopter: Igor Sikorsky. Yoghurt and longevity: Ilya Mechnikov (1910)."}];
let mode='tr', dir='en', unit='all', deck=[], i=0, known=0, seen=0;
function pool(){ const s = mode==='tr'?VOC:QA; return s.filter(x=>unit==='all'||x.u===unit).map(x=>({...x})); }
function restart(){ deck=pool(); i=0; known=0; seen=0; show(); }
function show(){
  document.getElementById('card').classList.remove('open');
  const c = deck[i];
  document.getElementById('meta').textContent = deck.length ? 'Карточка ' + (i+1) + ' из ' + deck.length : 'Нет карточек';
  if(!c){ document.getElementById('front').textContent='Готово!'; document.getElementById('back').textContent=''; document.getElementById('lblF').textContent=''; }
  else {
    document.getElementById('lblF').textContent = mode==='tr' ? (dir==='en' ? 'Слово (англ.)' : 'Слово (рус.)') : 'Вопрос';
    document.getElementById('front').textContent = mode==='tr' ? (dir==='en' ? c.en : c.ru) : c.q;
    document.getElementById('back').textContent = mode==='tr' ? (dir==='en' ? c.ru : c.en) : c.a;
  }
  document.getElementById('stats').textContent = 'Знаю: ' + known + ' из ' + seen;
}
function answer(ok){
  if(!deck.length) return;
  seen++;
  const c = deck[i];
  if(ok){ known++; deck.splice(i,1); if(i>=deck.length) i=0; }
  else { deck.splice(i,1); deck.push(c); if(i>=deck.length) i=0; }
  show();
}
function setMode(m){ mode=m; document.getElementById('m-tr').classList.toggle('on',m==='tr'); document.getElementById('m-qa').classList.toggle('on',m==='qa'); restart(); }
function setUnit(u){ unit=u; ['all','2','4'].forEach(x=>document.getElementById('u-'+x).classList.toggle('on',x===u)); restart(); }
function shuffle(){ for(let k=deck.length-1;k>0;k--){const j=Math.floor(Math.random()*(k+1));[deck[k],deck[j]]=[deck[j],deck[k]];} i=0; show(); }
function toggleDir(){ dir = dir==='en'?'ru':'en'; document.getElementById('dir').textContent = dir==='en'?'EN → RU':'RU → EN'; show(); }
restart();
</script>
